# Water systems' controls wiped through a shared remote-support platform: Clayton County, Georgia
variant "Clayton County, Georgia"
    summary "An AI agent wipes the controls of the water system of Clayton County, Georgia through the
        remote-support platform of the OT integrator that it uses."
    scope "The people that the water system of Clayton County, Georgia serves, the hospitals that it supplies
        and the businesses that need public water there."

clayton_county: Utility
    "Clayton County Water Authority, Georgia. Its ID in the federal inventory is GA0630000."
    buys = <>
    sells = <>
    name = "Clayton County Water Authority"
    demand = 39.333333 Mgal/day  @clayton_county_demand
    nameplate = 42 Mgal/day  @clayton_county_nameplate
    storage = 32.2 Mgal  @clayton_county_storage
    bought = 0 Mgal/day  @clayton_county_bought
    sold = 0.7 Mgal/day  @clayton_county_sold
    va_rate = 3086091.63 USD2025/h  @clayton_county_va_rate

clayton_county_homes: Households
    "The people that Clayton County Water Authority serves."
    utility = clayton_county
    population = 298374 people  @clayton_county_homes_population

clayton_county_hospitals: Hospitals
    "The hospital that Clayton County Water Authority supplies: Southern Regional Medical Center."
    utility = clayton_county
    name = "Clayton County Water Authority"
    facilities = 1 facilities  @clayton_county_hospitals_facilities

notes:
    limitations:
        on-demand-connections: "The Authority can open its connections with Atlanta, DeKalb and Fayette on
            demand, for about 8 and 2 Mgal a day. The model's purchases are the same at every hour, so it
            cannot open them when the Authority needs water, and the interruption can be longer here than in
            fact."  @clayton_county_bought
        capped-peak: "No source gives Clayton's highest day, and the median estimate is above its plants' 42
            Mgal a day, so the model takes that capacity as the peak. Water systems are sized for their
            highest day, so the true peak is probably lower, and the stored water can run out sooner here than
            in fact."  @clayton_county_demand
    verification:
        research: "variants/clayton/evidence/research.md"
    titles:
        clayton_county: "Clayton County Water Authority"
        clayton_county_homes: "Clayton County Water Authority: homes"
        clayton_county_hospitals: "Clayton County Water Authority: hospitals"

record clayton_county_demand: assumption "No source reports a highest day. The average production of 28.15 MGD
    times the family median ratio 1.54385 gives 43.4593 MGD, more than the 42 MGD that the three plants can
    produce, and the Authority buys no water on a normal day. So the peak is the plants' capacity; less the
    sale to College Park (0.7 MGD, taken as its peak-day amount), over 1.05: (42 - 0.7) / 1.05 = 39.3333."
    assumption A-SC43-PEAK-DAY
    claims:
        E-SC43-CLAYTON-CLAYTON-COUNTY-PLANTS
        E-SC43-CLAYTON-CLAYTON-COUNTY-COLLEGE-PARK

record clayton_county_nameplate: source "The three surface water plants: W.J. Hooper 20, J.W. Smith 12 and
    Terry R. Hicks 10 MGD, 42 MGD in all, the capacity that both the 2025 financial report and the 2026-2027
    budget give."
    claims:
        E-SC43-CLAYTON-CLAYTON-COUNTY-PLANTS
            datum: production_capacity
        E-SC43-CLAYTON-CLAYTON-COUNTY-SYSTEM
            datum: production_capacity

record clayton_county_storage: source "Three clear wells (6.7 MG), seven ground storage tanks (23 MG) and four
    elevated tanks (2.5 MG): 32.2 MG."
    claims:
        E-SC43-CLAYTON-CLAYTON-COUNTY-SYSTEM
            datum: storage_total
        E-SC43-CLAYTON-CLAYTON-COUNTY-PLANTS

record clayton_county_bought: source "The Authority produces its water from its reservoirs and the Flint
    River. Its six connections with Atlanta (about 8 MGD, or about 5 MGD in the 2025 report) and those with
    DeKalb and Fayette (up to 2 MGD) are for supply on demand, not routine purchases."
    claims:
        E-SC43-CLAYTON-CLAYTON-COUNTY-PLANTS
        E-SC43-CLAYTON-CLAYTON-COUNTY-INTERCONNECTIONS
        E-SC43-CLAYTON-CLAYTON-COUNTY-SYSTEM

record clayton_county_sold: assumption "College Park, a separate water system with a permanent connection from
    the Authority and a wholesale agreement, gets about 0.7 MGD from it according to a College Park document
    that could not be opened. The figure is taken as the peak-day sale, without the peak ratio, because it may
    be a capacity."
    assumption A-SC43-PEAK-DAY
    claims:
        E-SC43-CLAYTON-CLAYTON-COUNTY-COLLEGE-PARK
            datum: ccwa_supply_to_college_park
        E-SC43-CLAYTON-CLAYTON-COUNTY-BUYERS
        E-SC43-CLAYTON-CLAYTON-COUNTY-INTERCONNECTIONS

record clayton_county_homes_population: assumption "SDWIS federal data list 298,374 people served by Clayton
    County Water Authority (GA0630000), the system's own report through its state."
    assumption A-SC43-REACH
    claims:
        E-SC43-SDWIS-GA0630000
            datum: population_served

record clayton_county_va_rate: assumption "298,374 people served times the 2024 GDP per resident of its county
    (Clayton County, Georgia (13063): 26,281,522 thousand dollars and 297,703 people: 88,281.01 dollars a
    resident) times the price factor 1.026324, over 8,760 h: 3,086,091.63 USD2025/h."
    assumption A-SC43-ECONOMY
    claims:
        E-SC43-SDWIS-GA0630000
        E-SC43-SDWIS-COUNTIES
        E-SC43-BEA-GDP-13063
        E-SC43-BEA-POP-13063
        E-SC43-GDP-DEFLATOR

record clayton_county_hospitals_facilities: assumption "The CMS hospital list gives 1 hospital of the counted
    types whose geocoded address lies in the EPA service area of Clayton County Water Authority."
    assumption A-SC43-HOSPITAL-JOIN
    claims:
        E-SC43-HOSPITALS-CLAYTON-COUNTY
            datum: hospitals_supplied
